1. CAWDRON BROTHERS FARMING - STORAGE INVOICE (file LEG-2026-41)

Cawdron Brothers Farming, a member in the Tessaway Plains, has disputed invoice TPG-118204 for grain storage at the Harrowgate Elevator.

The facts are not in dispute. The member has 1,250 tonnes of barley in store. The invoice charges storage for 4 chargeable months (July, August, September and October 2026, invoiced in advance for October at the member's request) at the GH-04 rate that has applied since 1 July 2026.

The member says that because the barley was delivered in January 2026, the rate in the 2025 Season schedule should apply for as long as the grain stays in store. I do not agree. The current Grain Handling Fee Schedule says in terms that grain already in store on 1 July 2026 is charged at the 2026 rate from that date. The member was sent the new schedule on 12 June 2026.

The dispute was raised in writing 9 working days after the invoice date, so it is within the time allowed by the schedule and no interest runs while it is open.

My recommendation: hold the invoice in full. As a goodwill gesture, Member Services could offer to waive the GH-08 sampling fees on the member's next 10 loads. That offer would cost little and does not concede the point. Please confirm you are content and I will ask the Head of Member Services to write to the member.

2. BRAKESPEARE PACKAGING - CANCELLED CAN-END ORDER (file LEG-2026-37)

Brakespeare Packaging claims $143,275 for a cancelled order of easy-open can ends for the Pellam Bay Cannery. The order was cancelled on 3 June 2026 after the recall, when the butter bean production plan was cut.

Our position is that the supply agreement limits our liability on cancellation to the cost of materials already bought and not reusable, which on Brakespeare's own figures is $51,840. Brakespeare says the cap does not apply because we gave 16 days' notice and the agreement requires 30.

They have a point on notice. My assessment of our realistic exposure is between $51,840 and about $97,000.

Mediation is fixed for 21 October 2026 before Lysander Okoro of the Vessey Cross Mediation Chambers. Our share of the mediator's fee is $3,150.

I ask for authority to settle at mediation at any figure up to $78,500. That is above the limit of the General Counsel's delegation tier under the current Procurement and Purchasing Policy, so I understand the Chief Financial Officer's approval is needed as well. It is within the Chief Financial Officer's tier.

If we cannot settle, Brakespeare has said it will issue proceedings. The limitation period does not expire until June 2032, so there is no time pressure on their side. External counsel estimates our costs to trial at $66,000.

3. TIMING

- Cawdron Brothers: reply to the member due by 25 September 2026.
- Brakespeare: position paper to the mediator due by 9 October 2026; mediation 21 October 2026.

Neither matter is connected to the Inspectorate's enforcement notice, which the General Counsel is handling personally.

